  "pd": {
    "basis": "author-died-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Johann Pachelbel died in 1706; the Canon and Gigue in D major was composed c.1690 and first published from the Berlin manuscript in 1919. Free worldwide.",
    "sourceEdition": "Pachelbel, Kanon und Gigue in D-dur, first publication ed. Gustav Beckmann (1919), from the sole surviving manuscript in the Berlin State Library; Violin I part and continuo ground bass",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only; the Canon is wordless instrumental music. Written in common time with the ground bass moving in half notes, so the changes fall on half bars. Encoded at two bars per written bar (one ground-bass note per bar) at double the written tempo number, which makes the changes bar-granular as the engine requires and gives the melody a FINER grid, not a coarser one. The head is Variation 1, the descending line the first violin states over the first full turn of the ground; the later diminutions are not carried. Theme states that line an octave below written pitch, one note per bar; Theme Up states the same eight pitches at written pitch (F#5 down to A4), rearticulated on the written quarter-note pulse. That rearticulation is an editorial build on the same line, NOT Pachelbel's second variation, which is different material and is not carried here. This is the plain score line: not the Seiffert 1929 performing edition and not any modern pop arrangement."
  },
